nswer-field/id #uuid "36578da5-ae60-526e-8589-437da0b16644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "110d0b81-fd68-5dac-ba01-18cdf7c4cb65" :question/math-academy-id "q-113463" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{4(x-1)}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\frac{3}{4(x-1)},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/e80865b0f32a5473da27b39537034bcdabee0a6c24032f41413e20518642d3f4.png)\n\n- Translate it by $1$ unit to the right to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/8f2a42430ac31a559e5f85c961579aa482280bd9b69ed5d948250f5fafce21a5.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{4(x-1)}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/f558777e9ec384bdd169cddefeac589861cae2568f130816c4bf7a1f4ee1e520.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b305ee7-6f91-584b-86df-4be6f0e41fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/177afbf4d02a454a21815a021b4594d2e00867702909ab20f378695d2ec1d8b0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242254} {:answer/id #uuid "03ae6209-1d38-5b57-8430-924aeb685b35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/97cf018efcc6f4c7376de7bfe1d1c821c615666734c20e2c2d9cf15a54c11616.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242255} {:answer/id #uuid "ebda6465-2a7f-5092-b34b-2d8ac88aac6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/0fba58dd803485e67f90d0d4083c3c795d623d5c3f49defbde56ba6a04f50e7e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242256} {:answer/id #uuid "383b43fd-08f9-59ca-acaa-fcc9f07e4f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/3c8dd0e4726747b00e4377e43c06693326e10db2977257c9c20da4953127ddfd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242257} {:answer/id #uuid "a055ca54-797c-5018-afee-9ec2b830bb9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/23a24a5d620c86a06fdb520f0e2a5cb370d534773f5eed1d906b51fe5205b9e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebda6465-2a7f-5092-b34b-2d8ac88aac6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/0fba58dd803485e67f90d0d4083c3c795d623d5c3f49defbde56ba6a04f50e7e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242256} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc712bfa-d441-56de-b09f-120ed2f6aed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4fb552b9-23bd-5131-aeec-7c1788a3eec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113466" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{2}{x-2}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=-\\frac{2}{x-2},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/3c8dd0e4726747b00e4377e43c06693326e10db2977257c9c20da4953127ddfd.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{2}{x-2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/207dec9757b65a75c25ba7ace7a12bec8e922bda9018e58d6511d12de47426b7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "308f3685-0a90-56f3-9653-a83cec4286d2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Some Unknowns Given the Graph of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0a79572-4e5c-59da-ac61-e627dd869f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242261} {:answer/id #uuid "495a7717-e793-5533-a6a0-fdca52d57811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242262} {:answer/id #uuid "21fbe893-f299-5c1c-9c32-458a32ba26c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242263} {:answer/id #uuid "bf30b3ee-8be8-51fe-a1a2-b8230d0b0451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242264} {:answer/id #uuid "e2aa7b29-abd7-56f9-9a68-7248c200f040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2aa7b29-abd7-56f9-9a68-7248c200f040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242265} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7152e3c-502d-5c73-bde3-4eaafc52b585" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f44a540-d3ee-5911-b389-01c797246068" :question/math-academy-id "q-113476" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/c1159b59ac6a52c570852cb47ec1ef07d0985ee8b734f2e375cf54919c8b537b.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x}+k.$ What is the value of $a+k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=1.5.$ This means that $k=1.5$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x}+1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,3.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x}+1.5 \\\\ 3.5 & =\\frac{a}{1}+1.5 \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+k=2+1.5=3.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "308f3685-0a90-56f3-9653-a83cec4286d2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Some Unknowns Given the Graph of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a257671-f05a-5fb5-83cc-592d59e1bafd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242268} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd33a71-84fa-5ea4-bfee-feb76b5481f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242269} {:answer/id #uuid "2a18019e-214f-5477-835a-64e32c114c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242270} {:answer/id #uuid "2127ed7c-8fe5-5395-9db3-5f4b6d01207c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242271} {:answer/id #uuid "e4583fd6-6504-5fa8-9281-b9f0165b87af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4583fd6-6504-5fa8-9281-b9f0165b87af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242272} :answer-field/id #uuid "b59a60ad-efa6-5a68-9197-d1cb9cc79a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e98bb28-62ea-5816-b8f2-0d652d8602bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-113478" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/4f8e1c3db4120b0fcd8491a850d238b81452fb0294145a2062f96c4655164a04.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x}+k.$ What is the value of $a+k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=-1.5.$ This means that $k=-1.5$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x}-1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,-2)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x}-1.5 \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{a}{1}-1.5 \\\\ -0.5 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+k=-0.5-1.5=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "308f3685-0a90-56f3-9653-a83cec4286d2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Some Unknowns Given the Graph of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60082bf3-9cc3-5b4f-a861-831ce38e0678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242275} {:answer/id #uuid "434a8b7b-c127-5152-a15f-5c5d4b0419fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242276} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3249ec-4ce6-5a68-8f46-49e6df79cc6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242277} {:answer/id #uuid "77533ece-bdad-5eb2-87d5-83906f3edc6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242278} {:answer/id #uuid "1a2388b7-1b9a-5fb4-9f91-dd1ef1b59d60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "434a8b7b-c127-5152-a15f-5c5d4b0419fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242276} :answer-field/id #uuid "662589e3-a5e5-50aa-a3bd-abe792dd89a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68cd80d2-9b66-50c1-ba50-b4572464ab27" :question/math-academy-id "q-113482" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831126/assets/4d2dec8a05bc9835693c1df9dbc54618fd39d77667cbbdcd5bb6d92bf25c0d97.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What is the value of $a+b+k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=0.5,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=3.$ This means that $k=0.5$ and $b=3,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x-3}+0.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,1.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x-3}+0.5 \\\\ 1.5 & =\\frac{a}{1-3}+0.5 \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{a}{-2} \\\\ a & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+k=-2+3+0.5=1.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea2f1348-55bf-5313-8d5e-a5a097231e97" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:an